---
import DocLayout from '../../components/DocLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Workspace & IDE"
  description="Projects, the file explorer, the editor, the terminal, Git, language diagnostics, debugging, spaces, and the codebase index."
>
  <p>
    The workspace is a real developer environment, not a chat window
    bolted onto an editor. Everything below operates inside the folder
    you explicitly open.
  </p>

  <h2>Projects and files</h2>
  <p>
    Keep multiple projects with server-side scoping: thread lists filter
    by the active project, and opening a project reopens its folder.
    The explorer browses the tree; create, rename, and delete files and
    folders with unsaved-change protection. A scratch pad holds one-off
    code, and any file can be attached to chat as context.
  </p>

  <h2>The editor</h2>
  <p>
    Tabbed editing with syntax highlighting, line numbers,
    <code>Ctrl+S</code> atomic saves (temp file + rename), and
    <code>Tab</code> indentation. It is a focused text editor — not a
    full editor engine — and it never executes code itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Terminal and Git</h2>
  <p>
    A real PTY terminal follows the workspace directory. Git operations
    (status, commit, merge, rebase, worktrees) are available from the
    source-control view, and agent runs can hand their worktree back to
    you for the final commit.
  </p>

  <h2>Diagnostics and debugging</h2>
  <p>
    Language servers provide live diagnostics in the Problems panel:
    configure a server per language (or point at a saved binary when it
    is not on PATH), and errors stream in as you edit. The Debug panel
    speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol for launch-style debugging.
    Attach-style debugging depends on the adapter's own protocol shape
    and is still being hardened — treat it as experimental.
  </p>

  <h2>Index, memory, spaces</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Codebase index.</strong> A bounded index of the open folder
      with symbol search. Search by words always works; search by
      meaning needs an embedding model (router or <code>local:</code>),
      and symbols are embedded once and cached.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Project memory.</strong> Decisions, preferences, tasks, and
      facts you record are retrieved into chat and agent runs when they
      share words with the message — and the reply says which records it
      was given.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Spaces.</strong> Saved working contexts you can switch
      between without losing threads.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confinement</h2>
  <p>
    Workspace paths are canonicalised; <code>..</code> escapes, absolute
    paths outside the folder, and symlinks leaving it are rejected.
    Binary files and files over 1&nbsp;MB are refused by the editor API,
    and workspace routes reject non-loopback hosts and foreign origins.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
